\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Executive Summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 The identity industry has long focussed on managing humans, their account lifecycle and runtime access. A lot of these processes are based on traditional static controls. With the scale out of cloud infrastructure, and automation, these systems are not well positioned to handle machine accounts, and attackers are exploiting them in successful attacks. Non-Human Identities (NHIs) now outnumber humans by 100s:1 and increasing, and so too is the attack surface exploitable by these attackers. Agentic AI programs incorporate both humans and machines in end to end workflows, and with that brings both a cyber risk and an accountability gap. If not closed, organization\'92s face a crisis of accountability - as they will never be able to prove, with non-repudiation and integrity, a person was responsible for consequence of action.\
\
The emergence of agentic artificial intelligence ( Agentic AI) has fundamental implications for the existing paradigms of identity and access management (IAM). Unlike traditional software applications that operate within static parameters, Agentic AI systems exhibit autonomy, including the ability to independently invoke tools, negotiate cross-domain resource access, and execute transactional workflows on behalf of users. Customers expect solutions to operate within their existing technological landscape - which necessitates an interoperable approach that works at speed and with safety.\
\
A full end to end identity security strategy demands a number of elements in order to deliver the runtime security accountability:\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Unified Identity Fabric: Bridging human and NHI management into a single plane for a centralized audit trail.
\fs24 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Discovery & Inventory: Automatically identifying all service accounts, bots, and AI agents across cloud and on-prem systems.
\fs24 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Automated Lifecycle Management: Implementing standardized creation with templates and clear human ownership tagging.
\fs24 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
AI-Powered Posture Management: Using real-time risk scoring to detect behavioral drift in agents.
\fs24 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Just-in-Time, Just-Enough Access: Converting long-lived NHIs into demand-driven entities with precision-scoped permissions.
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
This shift necessitates a transition from human-centric authentication models to a security architecture capable of supporting phishing resistant authentication, complex delegation, fine-grained authorization, full traceability in human and account owners and cryptographically bound identity.\'a0\
\
If this is rationalised down to the runtime security elements, the core requirements are implemented by supporting advanced WebAuthn, OpenID Connect (OIDC) and OAuth 2.0 extensions.\'a0 Differentiation between major identity providers\'97e.g. IBM Verify and Microsoft Entra ID\'97is increasingly exposed as these scenarios are deployed within client environments.\
\
Since these technical standards exist today, we take this opportunity to explore the critical decision points on implementation. This paper explores that runtime access management element. It does this within the context of a typical enterprise, one which has subscribed to a Microsoft Entra ID entitlement. While Microsoft Entra ID remains a ubiquitous solution for general workforce identity, its architecture is frequently constrained by proprietary implementations due to resistance in adopting emerging IETF standards. Conversely, IBM has demonstrated, and continues to advocate for strong open standards. IBM Verify demonstrates this by providing a robust standards-compliant framework for securing the high-churn, distributed environments typical of agentic AI.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 The Identity Imperative in Agentic AI Ecosystems\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 The security requirements for agentic AI extend significantly beyond those of conventional applications. Traditional OAuth 2.0 and OIDC flows assume a synchronous user session where a human provides real-time consent.\'a0Agentic AI, however, frequently operates in headless, background, or decoupled environments where such interactions are impractical.\'a0Furthermore, agents often initiate recursive delegation chains requiring a specific subset of the original user's permissions.\'a0In such environments, the principle of least privilege is a functional requirement for mitigating the risks of privilege escalation and autonomous decision-making errors.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\
Standard bearer tokens, where simple possession is sufficient for authorization, present an unacceptable risk. If a bearer token is leaked from an agent\'92s cache or intercepted during transit, it can be replayed by an adversary without cryptographic proof. To address this, agentic systems require sender-constraining mechanisms like DPoP (Demonstration of Proof-of-Possession) to ensure tokens are bound to the client. Additionally, coarse-grained OAuth scopes are insufficient for agents performing specific, parameterized tasks. The emergence of Rich Authorization Requests (RAR) provides the framework necessary for expressing these fine-grained intentions.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Technical Superiority of IBM Verify in Advanced OIDC Standards\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 IBM Verify\'92s value lies in its "API-first" and "standards-first" directive, leading to the implementation of multiple OIDC extensions that Microsoft Entra ID has either not adopted or has implemented in a limited capacity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Dynamic and Granular Authorization: Rich Authorization Requests (RAR)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 IBM Verify supports OAuth 2.0 Rich Authorization Requests (RFC 9396), allowing clients to request detailed attributes during authorization.\'a0In an agentic AI scenario, an agent might need permission to "Execute a transfer of $100 from Account A to Account B" rather than a blanket "Execute transfers" permission.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
IBM Verify\'92s RAR implementation enables the authorization server to make informed decisions through configuration parameters:\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
authorization_details_types_supported: Defines specific types the server can process, such as\'a0account_information\'a0or\'a0payment_initiation.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
authorization_details_types_schema: Leverages JSON schema files to validate incoming request details, ensuring necessary transactional context is provided before consent.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
identifier_strategy: Allows custom functions to compute unique identifiers for complex authorization details.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
In contrast, Microsoft Entra ID does not support the OAuth 2.0 RAR specification.\'a0Entra ID remains restricted to string-based scopes that must be pre-registered in the app manifest, currently forcing developers to over-permission their AI agents because arbitrary JSON structures cannot be handled at runtime.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Cryptographic Sender Constraining: Demonstration of Proof-of-Possession (DPoP)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 DPoP (RFC 9449) is essential for preventing token replay by cryptographically binding the access token to a client-generated key pair. IBM Verify provides native support for DPoP across all grant types, including\'a0authorization_code\'a0and\'a0client_credentials.\'a0The process involves:\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Key Generation: The agent generates an asymmetric key pair.
\fs24 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
DPoP Proof: The agent includes a\'a0DPoP\'a0header containing a signed JWT with a public key and claims such as\'a0htu\'a0(HTTP Target URI) and\'a0htm\'a0(HTTP Method).
\fs24 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Token Binding: IBM Verify validates the proof and embeds a public key thumbprint in the\'a0cnf\'a0(confirmation) claim of the token. The thumbprint is calculated as:
\fs24 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl1
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
jkt=Base64URL(SHA-256(JWK_Public_Key))
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
Microsoft Entra ID\'92s PoP implementation is primarily optimized for public clients running on Windows using the Windows Account Manager (WAM) broker. PoP for confidential clients (server-side agents) is considered limited and platform-constrained and lacks support for multi-platform environments, creating a platform-dependent model that is less suitable for modern, containerized AI workloads.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Decoupled Interaction: Client-Initiated Backchannel Authentication (CIBA)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 A critical requirement for agentic AI is triggering authentication when the user is not present at the console. CIBA allows a client to trigger an authentication flow without front-channel redirects.\'a0IBM Verify supports CIBA with\'a0Poll\'a0and\'a0Ping\'a0modes, allowing an agent to securely initiate a push challenge (via the\'a0/oauth2/ciba\'a0endpoint) to the user\'92s mobile device to authorize a high-value action. Entra ID lacks a standardized OIDC CIBA implementation for third-party developers, relying instead on proprietary notification systems within its own ecosystem.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Secure Metadata: Pushed Authorization Requests (PAR)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 Pushed Authorization Requests (PAR - RFC 9126) address URL leakage by requiring the client to push the authorization payload to a secure back-channel endpoint. IBM Verify implements PAR as a core feature, receiving a\'a0request_uri\'a0in return. This ensures integrity, confidentiality, and scalability for complex requests. PAR support exists in limited SDK and framework contexts and is not uniformly enforced across the Entra ID platform.\

