\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Identity Propagation: RFC 8693 vs. Entra OBO\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 The most critical differentiator in multi-hop agentic security is how identity is propagated across services. IBM Verify adheres to the IETF\'a0RFC 8693 OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange\'a0standard, while Entra ID relies on its proprietary\'a0On-Behalf-Of (OBO)\'a0flow.\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\
Limitations of Microsoft Entra OBO Flow\
Microsoft\'92s OBO flow suffers from several architectural constraints that hinder agentic security:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Proprietary and Non-Interoperable: OBO is a custom Microsoft implementation. This creates a "walled garden" where agents cannot easily propagate identity to third-party services (e.g., non-Azure APIs) without building custom "shim" layers.
\fs24 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Token Bloat and Context Leakage: OBO typically replicates the user's entire identity context into the downstream token. This "token bloat" often leads to over-permissioning because the downstream service receives the user's full context rather than a purpose-scoped subset.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Invisibility of the Actor: In the OBO model, the intermediate agent is often "invisible" in the new token. The downstream resource sees the user's identity but lacks a structured, cryptographically-bound way to identify the specific agent that initiated the request.
\fs24 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Service Principal Barrier: Entra's OBO flow is primarily designed for user principals. If an agent authenticates using a service principal, it cannot use OBO to call another API while maintaining user context; it must fallback to a client-credentials grant, which completely loses the link to the originating user.
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
Advantages of IBM Verify Token Exchange (RFC 8693)\
IBM Verify provides a true Security Token Service (STS) that implements the full RFC 8693 specification, offering significantly more flexibility and security:\'a0\'a0\'a0\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Subject and Actor Separation: The exchange allows for a\'a0subject_token\'a0(representing the user) and an\'a0actor_token\'a0(representing the agent).\'a0The resulting token includes an\'a0act\'a0claim, ensuring total auditability of who acted for whom.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The\'a0may_act\'a0Claim: IBM Verify supports the\'a0may_act\'a0claim, which can be embedded in the original user token to define exactly which agents or clients are authorized to perform an exchange on behalf of that user.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Cross-Domain Trust: Tokens can be exchanged across different trust domains, allowing an agent to swap a token from one provider for a token issued by IBM Verify to call protected enterprise resources.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Runtime Policy Evaluation: IBM Verify can evaluate an access policy as part of the token exchange flow, allowing for finer-grained authorization checks than a static scope mapping can provide.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Securing Interaction Models: User 2 Agent vs. Agent 2 Agent\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 Agentic security requires different strategies based on whether a human is the ultimate driver or if agents are collaborating autonomously.\
\
User-to-Agent (U2A) Model\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In the U2A model (e.g., a "Copilot"), the primary goal is\'a0delegated authority.
\fs24 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Granular Consent: IBM Verify uses\'a0RAR\'a0to show the user exactly what the agent will do (e.g., "Allow Agent to move file X to location Y").
\fs24 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Decoupled Step-up: If an agent attempts a sensitive action,\'a0CIBA\'a0is used to "wake up" the user on their mobile device for a just-in-time approval, even if the agent is operating as a background task.
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
Agent-to-Agent (A2A) Model\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In the A2A model (e.g., an orchestration agent calling a sub-agent), the primary goal is\'a0cryptographic accountability.
\fs24 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Interoperable Messaging: As agents adopt the\'a0Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol\'a0(emerging A2A specifications under Linux Foundation stewardship), IBM Verify\'92s support for\'a0Token Exchange\'a0ensures that identity is preserved as tasks pass between disparate agent frameworks.\'a0\'a0\'a0
\fs24 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Chain of Custody: By using\'a0RFC 8693, A2A interactions maintain a nested actor chain. A downstream service receiving a request from "Sub-Agent B" can trace the authority back to "Master Agent A" and finally to the human user, preventing anonymous lateral movement in the network.
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Shared Signals and Continuous Risk: The Antenna Framework\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 Agentic security requires a dynamic response to changing risk postures.\'a0IBM Verify Antenna\'a0is a lightweight, self-hosted container that enables the exchange of security events using the\'a0Shared Signals Framework (SSF).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
As a Transmitter: Antenna ingests data from sources (audit streams, databases) and converts them into standardized Security Event Tokens (SETs) to broadcast to receivers.
\fs24 \
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
As a Receiver: It consumes SETs (e.g., "session-revoked", "risk-level-changed") and converts them into immediate actions, such as revoking agent sessions when anomalous behavior is detected.
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf0 This allows for real-time revocation of a rogue agent\'92s access. Microsoft Entra ID\'92s Continuous Access Evaluation (CAE) is robust but primarily optimized for its own first-party services and Windows endpoints, lacking the modular flexibility of Antenna for cross-vendor environments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Extended IAM Controls and IBM-HashiCorp Synergy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs32 \cf0 Beyond protocol support, IBM provides a comprehensive suite of IAM controls and infrastructure security:\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Identity Protection (VIP): Uses AI/ML to analyze network logs and telemetry, discovering "shadow" assets and directories that often harbor unmanaged agents.
\fs24 \
\ls9\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Dynamic Credentials with Vault: With HashiCorp now an IBM company,\'a0HashiCorp Vault\'a0issues short-lived, just-in-time credentials for AI applications, removing the risk of hard-coded static secrets.
\fs24 \
\ls9\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Vault Radar: Scans source code and configuration files for exposed secrets (cloud keys, API tokens) before an agent is deployed.
\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

